search reset stack Grokking UnitTests Utilities list;
register(ProbeR);
define
    iL;
    iRun code={
        cerr "-- A direct subscript, twice: same node?":;
        canonOf(Grokking["Braced"]);
        canonOf(Grokking["Braced"]);
        cerr "-- B via a <- local:":;
        iL <- Grokking["Braced"];
        canonOf(iL);
        cerr "IDENT SENTINEL":;
        stop();
        };
    ;
iRun();

THE CONFOUNDER, MEASURED 2026-08-22 (Clay dispatch amendment 9)

    A  canonOf(Grokking["Braced"])   face=0x10110c300 canon=0x10110c300  SAME NODE
       (twice, identical)
    B  iL <- Grokking["Braced"]
       canonOf(iL)                   face=0x10118c080 canon=0x10110c300  DIFFERENT NODES

⚠ `<-` DOES NOT HAND YOU THE NODE. It hands you a COPY whose definingRule()
still resolves to the original. The direct subscript DOES hand you the node,
and the same node every time.

WHY THIS MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS: a copy answers every question about the
original correctly. Same tag, same flags, same list, same definingRule. It
reads identically. It only diverges when you MUTATE it -- and then the write
lands on the copy and every read-back off that copy CONFIRMS THE WRITE. The
probe reports success and the system never changed.

WHAT IT COST TODAY, and every one of these was reported before it was caught:
  - "the catalog face and canon are DIFFERENT NODES"  -- WRONG. That was the
    <- copy vs canon. The catalog node IS canon.
  - "setParse does not move the action" (finding 1)   -- withdrawn earlier for
    a different reason; also contaminated by this.
  - "commissioning breaks setParse" (finding 3)       -- WRONG. Does not
    reproduce when the real node is used.
  - "the fork reads null" (finding 2)                 -- WRONG as a defect. The
    fork reads null because the binding went to a copy.

THE RULE: to MUTATE a node, name it directly -- Grokking["X"], or an iterate
cursor. Reserve `<-` for values you only read. And when a bind "succeeds" but
its effect is invisible elsewhere, suspect that you bound a copy before you
suspect the mechanism.
